Mean Sensitivity: One Bad Star
============

Group: Further foundations

Open index.html directly (file://), alongside the sibling TeachingTools
directory, or serve both statically. No R, Shiny, WebR, external libraries,
install, build or network is needed for the HTML page. The original app.R
and model.R remain unchanged.

Lecture:
https://anguswright.github.io/LectureNotes/IntroductionToStatistics_Section1b_html.html

Explore
-------
The clean population is Normal(0,1). Choose 20-120 measurements and replace
0-10 of them with velocities near a controllable -12 to +12 km/s centre
(fixed Gaussian offsets of SD 0.1). Drag the orange handle or an orange
observation; the velocity slider provides the same accessible keyboard
control. Clean sample, one extreme star and many-contaminant presets retain
the original examples and lecture guidance.

The summary bars are mean +/- sample SD and median +/- Gaussian-scaled
MAD. They describe location and dispersion, NOT confidence intervals or
standard errors. SD uses n-1. The median averages the two central values
for an even sample; nMAD=median(abs(x-median(x)))/qnorm(0.75), with no
additional finite-sample correction. Gaussian consistency does not make
nMAD equal the SD of an arbitrary non-Gaussian population.

The added trimmed mean removes floor(n*trim) observations from each tail
of the sorted sample, as R's mean(x, trim=trim) does. It is not a procedure
that selectively removes only the orange points.

Intentional HTML differences
---------------------------
* Adds real pointer/touch dragging, a trim-fraction control (0-0.4 per tail,
  default 0.1), trimmed-mean metric and location marker.
* Adds finite-sample contamination-response curves for mean, median and
  trimmed mean. These are not asymptotic influence functions. The mean's
  slope is replacements/n; resistance can fail with enough contaminants.
* Keeps a wide velocity axis so dragging the same replacement cluster is
  easy to interpret. Summary bars and observations remain in view.
* Uses Astro.createRng(seed), not R's RNG, so identical seed numbers yield
  different individual draws. Caches 120 clean measurements, ten offsets
  and 120 jitter values. Moving contaminants/trimming never changes them;
  increasing n uses the same prefix. Dragging after New realisation does
  not reset its generation.
* Seed supports the shared browser range 0 through 2147483647.

Mean and median can describe different properties of asymmetric
populations. Robustness is not permission to discard a scientifically real
secondary population. Explain makes these qualifications explicit.

Model API
---------
model.js is pure CommonJS/browser code (RobustStatisticsModel globally):
  trimmedMean(values, trim=0.1)
    Two-sided floor(n*trim) trimming; does not mutate input order.
  summaries(values, trim=0.1)
    Returns {mean, median, sd, nmad, trimmed} for at least two finite values.
  validate(parameters)
    Checks seed, position, contaminants, n and trim using ValidationError.
  prepare(seed)
    Returns {seed, clean, offsets, jitter}, independent of display controls.
  contaminate(clean, offsets, count, position)
    Returns a copy replacing the first count observations, not appending.
  compute(parameters, prepared?)
    Returns data, parameters, clean, observed, jitter, summaries,
    cleanSummaries and 121 contamination-response rows in influence.

Files and validation
--------------------
Runtime: index.html, model.js, app.js, plus
../TeachingTools/{statistics.js,browser.js,browser.css,lesson.js}.
No extra assets are needed; README and tests are not runtime files.

From the repository root, with Node 22+ and Chrome installed:
  node --test RobustStatistics/test_model.js
  env -u HTML_SITE_ROOT TMPDIR="$PWD/RobustStatistics" node RobustStatistics/test_browser.js

Numerical tests include R-calibrated odd/even medians, SD and scaled MAD;
noninteger trim counts; contamination replacement and analytic mean
response; isolated extremes; half-sample contamination; fixed data/jitter;
and invalid inputs. Real Chrome tests move the actual handle with mouse
events, check both drag limits and unchanged clean observations, exercise
every control/boundary, preserve a new realisation through dragging, and
cover plot effects, presets/reset, validation, keyboard tabs, mobile and
offline file loading.
